00:00:09.059 --> 00:00:11.150
The Arctic is warming.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:15.329
That is not an assumption, a belief, a theory,
or a political stance.

00:00:15.329 --> 00:00:18.880
That is an empirical fact based on empirical
data.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:23.779
Just in 2018, based off the average between
1958 and 2002 the yearly average temperature

00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:27.639
in the Arctic should have been this, but in
reality it was this.

00:00:27.639 --> 00:00:33.820
One year is not a trend but here was 2017,
here was 2016, 2015, 2014, and 2013.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:40.890
In 2017, overall annual Arctic air temperatures
were 2.9 degrees Fahrenheit or 1.6 degrees

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:41.890
Celsius above average.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:46.290
That may not seem like much but that small
difference is like if Paris’ climate changed

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.050
to that of Istanbul.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:51.800
Humans have lived in the Arctic for tens of
thousands of years, it’s far from impossible,

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:52.970
but it is difficult.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:57.110
We’re no strangers to living in difficult
places—humans survive on islands days away

00:00:57.110 --> 00:01:01.370
from civilization; in deserts nearly completely
devoid of natural resources; and in places

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:05.830
like the Arctic where cold and darkness create
some of the most rugged conditions on earth.

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:09.290
At least some of us seem to enjoy living in
tough conditions.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.080
Where humans are lazy, in a sense, though,
is economically.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:16.530
When there’s an option to get the same for
less, we’ll almost always take it.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:20.670
The Arctic is an enormous area with plenty
of natural and human resources but it’s

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:25.870
also a harsh and isolated place meaning there’s
always been the same options for less, at

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:27.500
least until now.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:31.200
Traditionally, the Arctic has been far from
a center of economic activity.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.840
For example, the Canadian territory of Nunavut
has a gross domestic product of just $2 billion

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:36.840
USD.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.380
That means that, despite being the largest
province or territory composing 21% of Canada’s

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:46.340
land area, Nunavut only makes up 0.13% of
the national economy.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.729
The same is true elsewhere in the Arctic.

00:01:48.729 --> 00:01:54.020
Alaska makes up 16% of the US’ land but
only 0.27% of its economy.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:59.440
The Finnish region of Lapland makes up 27%
of the country’s land but only 2.5% of its

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:00.440
economy.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:05.020
In perhaps the most staggering example, Greenland
has a physical size larger than that of Mexico

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:09.030
and yet its economy is smaller than that of
the tiny island country of Aruba.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:14.489
However, its this very desolation, both physically
and economically, that’s spurring new Arctic

00:02:14.489 --> 00:02:15.680
industries.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.400
Emptiness is an asset.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.769
This space is being filled by those seeking
isolation.

00:02:20.769 --> 00:02:25.269
Tourism has established itself as the first
of two major sectors growing in the Arctic.

00:02:25.269 --> 00:02:29.610
For example, Finland’s Lapland region has
seen double digit tourism growth over recent

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:30.610
years.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.480
This area has particularly benefited from
the attention it receives from Europe’s

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.500
package holiday industry.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:38.830
Particularly in the month leading up to Christmas,
charter flights by companies like Thomas Cook

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:43.560
and TUI will leave from nearly every UK airport
to far north Lapland airports like Kittilä,

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.620
Rovaniemi, and Ivalo.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:48.840
Tourists come to visit destinations like Santa
Claus Village, an amusement park that bills

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:53.079
itself as the home of Santa, as well as engaging
in outdoors activities such as snowmobiling,

00:02:53.079 --> 00:02:54.709
dogsledding, and skiing.

00:02:54.709 --> 00:02:58.950
With the relative ease of visiting these destinations
via direct flights from destinations around

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:03.599
Europe and especially the UK, package tour
companies even sell day-trips to the Arctic.

00:03:03.599 --> 00:03:07.180
One flies out from somewhere like London in
the morning, visits Santa Claus Village, and

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:08.879
flies back in the evening.

00:03:08.879 --> 00:03:13.230
Of course, this tourism industry, despite
its rapid growth, has its shortcomings.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:18.249
About 44% of all yearly arrivals happen in
December and January making it highly seasonal

00:03:18.249 --> 00:03:22.459
so the region has put efforts into attracting
more spring, summer, and fall visitors.

00:03:22.459 --> 00:03:26.689
Lapland has nonetheless decided that tourism
is likely its most promising driver of growth

00:03:26.689 --> 00:03:30.590
so it has placed this industry at the forefront
of its economic development efforts.

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:35.010
This promise of tourism has been identified
by other Arctic areas, as well.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:39.129
Visits are increasing steadily in Alaska,
the Yukon, the Northwest Territories, Nunavut,

00:03:39.129 --> 00:03:42.819
Svalbard, it’s tough to find an Arctic region
where tourism isn't growing.

00:03:42.819 --> 00:03:46.879
While at a much smaller scale than places
like Alaska or Lapland, Greenland has made

00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:49.760
moderately successful efforts to expand tourism
as well.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.629
It has seen healthy growth rates from 181,000
nights stayed by tourists 20 years ago to

00:03:54.629 --> 00:03:56.779
263,000 today.

00:03:56.779 --> 00:04:00.739
Like many places in the Arctic, though, it
suffers from accessibility issues.

00:04:00.739 --> 00:04:03.339
It’s just hard to get to Greenland.

00:04:03.339 --> 00:04:07.299
There are really only two ways to travel there
from the outside world—via the three hour

00:04:07.299 --> 00:04:11.549
turboprop flight from Reykjavik or the four
hour jet flight from Copenhagen.

00:04:11.549 --> 00:04:15.680
Both options are expensive with the flight
from Reykjavik or Copenhagen to Nuuk, Greenland’s

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:19.169
capital, costing around, at best, $800.

00:04:19.169 --> 00:04:21.319
There are no day trips to Greenland.

00:04:21.319 --> 00:04:24.830
This is part of the reason why much of the
growth in tourism both in Greenland and the

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:28.770
rest of the Arctic uses another means of transport—cruise
ships.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:32.320
In another video we’ve covered how the warming
of the arctic is melting sea-ice and opening

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:33.909
up new maritime routes.

00:04:33.909 --> 00:04:38.200
This is making it far easier for large cruise
ships to sail through Arctic waters into small

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:39.580
northern towns.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:43.360
Now, Arctic cruises have happened for decades
to a certain extent but in the past they were

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:48.270
always done by ships like this—small expedition
style vessels with strengthened hulls to withstand

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:49.270
ice.

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:53.750
In 2016, though, the beast of Arctic cruises—the
northwest passage route through the Canadian

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:59.370
archipelago from the Pacific to the Atlantic—was
completed by this ship—the Crystal Serenity.

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:03.189
Thanks to the lower ice levels, it’s now
possible to sail large, traditional ships

00:05:03.189 --> 00:05:04.660
through this route.

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:08.780
The Crystal Serenity is a regular cruise ship
built with a regular hull just like every

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:09.870
other cruise ship.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:13.830
Unlike the smaller ships of before, this one
carried 1,000 passengers through the high

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:19.300
Arctic in comfort with onboard restaurants,
a spa, theatre, casino, and more.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:24.040
When making voyages like this, ships typically
make stops in some of the tiny Alaskan, Canadian,

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:26.580
and Greenlandic towns along the route.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:29.849
One of the most popular stops is the town
of Pond Inlet.

00:05:29.849 --> 00:05:34.169
Normally the only way in here is via the three
hour flight from Iqaluit that costs well over

00:05:34.169 --> 00:05:39.229
$1,000 roundtrip but a few times a year tourists
just wake up a couple hundred feet off its

00:05:39.229 --> 00:05:40.229
shore.

00:05:40.229 --> 00:05:44.729
Only 1,600 people live in Pond Inlet and yet
in 2018 eighteen cruise ships stopped in the

00:05:44.729 --> 00:05:47.060
town carrying over 3,200 passengers.

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:52.260
That’s up from ten ships in 2015, nine in
2016, and thirteen in 2017.

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:57.719
For a small town of 1,600, especially one
like Pond Inlet with a 22% unemployment rate,

00:05:57.719 --> 00:06:01.389
the significance of thousands of the kind
of tourists willing to pay tens of thousands

00:06:01.389 --> 00:06:03.770
for an Arctic cruise visiting is enormous.

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:07.750
The local governments runs programs to help
develop businesses to cater to cruise guests,

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:11.719
stores stock up on products to sell to visitors,
and dozens gain temporary employment to help

00:06:11.719 --> 00:06:13.750
coordinate the tourist’s shore visits.

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:17.660
Then, on a rapid-fire basis in August and
early September, the only time period when

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:21.830
the northwest passage is navigable, ships
show up just off the coast of Pond Inlet and

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:25.990
dozens of other northern Canadian and Alaskan
towns every few days.

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:32.800
The economic benefit of these visits is indisputable—Pond
Inlet earned $250,000 in docking fees in 2018

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:35.870
in addition to the benefit local businesses
and employees received.

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:38.020
It’s not all good news, though.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:42.090
Due to the enormous price of shipping goods
to Arctic towns like Pond Inlet, most of the

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:46.930
indigenous residents hunt whale, seal, caribou,
and other indigenous animals to feed their

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:47.930
families.

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:50.880
There’s little hard research on its effect
yet, but many are concerned about these large

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:53.939
ships driving away animals and changing hunting
grounds.

00:06:53.939 --> 00:06:57.220
This is in addition to the effect climate
change itself is already having.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:01.639
There are also some concerned about poorer
air quality from ship exhaust, pollution from

00:07:01.639 --> 00:07:05.520
ship discharge, for the safety of passengers
in an emergency considering the limited search

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:09.599
and rescue capability, and with the preservation
of these local cultures.

00:07:09.599 --> 00:07:13.350
Just like the nature around them, the isolation
of these towns has kept the traditions and

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:17.159
customs of these communities alive in the
modern era and one can imagine what it’s

00:07:17.159 --> 00:07:21.280
like to have 1,000 tourists suddenly show
up in a town of 1,600 that’s used to having,

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:24.259
at most, a few dozen outsiders there at a
time.

00:07:24.259 --> 00:07:28.169
The Arctic wants economic development, but
they don’t want these towns to turn into

00:07:28.169 --> 00:07:29.690
tourist destinations.

00:07:29.690 --> 00:07:34.249
Most want the cruise ships to come, but they
also want to keep the surroundings unspoiled.

00:07:34.249 --> 00:07:38.099
One comes at the cost of the other so it’s
all just a balancing act of interests.

00:07:38.099 --> 00:07:42.990
There are, though, more heavily weighted scales
balancing the economy and the environment.

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:48.820
According to estimates, this area, the Arctic,
holds 13% of the world’s undiscovered oil.

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:54.110
That represents 90 billion barrels which,
at current prices, are worth more than $4

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:55.110
trillion.

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:59.080
The thing is, it doesn’t really make sense
to drill in the Arctic right now.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:03.580
That’s because, in the Arctic, to drill
that barrel of oil which, in January, 2019,

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:06.960
is selling for $46, it costs about $78.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:11.500
That’s compared to costs as low as $29 per
barrel with onshore drilling in the Middle

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:12.500
East.

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:15.629
Whereas in the Middle East all you have to
do is build an oil rig, in the Arctic, where

00:08:15.629 --> 00:08:19.849
there’s limited infrastructure, for offshore
drilling, one has to build a rig, get icebreaker

00:08:19.849 --> 00:08:23.849
ships, establish a port, set up a transportation
and supply network, and more.

00:08:23.849 --> 00:08:28.080
In some ways, therefore, Arctic ice melt is
helping reduce these costs as it’s easier

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:31.889
for ships to move around both to explore for
oil and serve rigs.

00:08:31.889 --> 00:08:36.340
In other ways, though, this is adding difficulties
as less ice leads to larger waves which require

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:40.190
bigger, more expensive boats to navigate which
sometimes require dredging in the shallow

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:42.200
waters of some oil-rich locations.

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:46.870
Right now, Arctic oil drilling is sort-of
in a holding pattern until oil prices increase

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:50.690
or drilling costs decrease enough for it to
become economically viable.

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:54.710
As a whole, though, in the long-term, Arctic
warming decreases cost for the oil and gas

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:59.630
industry as it makes the area look and act
more like the cheaper and warmer south.

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:03.440
The economic impact for other industries is
more of a mixed bag, though.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:07.970
For example, fishing represents a large proportion
of the current Arctic economy and, while some

00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:12.330
species of fish will grow in number due to
warmer waters others will slowly disappear.

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:15.800
In mining, the opening of sea routes will
make transportation cheaper but the industry

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:19.430
actually benefits from having the frozen,
permafrost land which is disappearing since

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:23.580
it gives a stable platform for equipment and
therefore makes set-up less expensive.

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:27.790
In fact, land transportation infrastructure
in general, including airports, is expected

00:09:27.790 --> 00:09:31.910
to deteriorate rapidly as permafrost melts
and land becomes less stable.

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:35.740
The tourism industry, which does benefit from
the easier access and increased activity in

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:38.530
the Arctic, suffers too from Arctic warming.

00:09:38.530 --> 00:09:42.730
In November, 2018, Lapland lacked any snow
due to unseasonably high temperatures which

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:46.420
led to mass trip cancellations by tourists
who booked their trips to see the traditionally

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:48.040
snowy Arctic.

00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:51.720
Tourists come for the winter weather so a
future without snow in the region is potentially

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:54.370
a future without tourism in Lapland.

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:59.370
In addition, the pristine natural environment
is the asset of the Arctic tourism industry

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:04.500
so an Arctic without that pristine natural
environment is one without its tourism industry.

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:08.800
Even ignoring the irreversible, disastrous
consequences the warming of the Arctic is

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:13.400
already having on polar bears and caribou
and fish and walruses and reindeer and all

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:18.860
the other native Arctic species, this circle
is home to four million people.

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:23.650
The Arctic plus a few more degrees is an Arctic
that has fundamentally changed the way these

00:10:23.650 --> 00:10:28.660
four million people live and work and that’s
an Arctic that may no longer be truly home

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:29.660
to them.

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:33.620
The people, animals, and environment of the
Arctic, in a metaphorical sense, are situated

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:37.240
between two, massive, slow-moving icebergs.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:41.330
One is the power of climate change and the
other is the the power of the economy.

00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:45.990
Climate change could possibly be slowed or
stopped or reversed but only with great effort

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:48.440
by more than just the people of the Arctic.

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:53.590
On the other side, the power of economic pushes
and pulls is perhaps just as difficult to

00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:54.590
stop.

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:58.400
If it makes economic sense for industries
to be in the Arctic, they’re almost certainly

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:02.440
going to be in the Arctic even if it means
environmental ruin.

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:06.161
These two massive, slow-moving icebergs are
on their collision course with the Arctic

00:11:06.161 --> 00:11:09.810
already and, it might just be too late to
stop them.

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:14.830
The physical, economic, and social landscape
of the Arctic is going to change.

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:17.160
This might be a story without a happy ending.

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:21.301
The only positive note to end on is that,
while riding the sinking ship, there’s at

00:11:21.301 --> 00:11:25.890
least a chance to make money on the way down.

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:29.660
If you ever get the chance to visit the Arctic,
which, as someone who’s been there, I highly

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:33.540
recommend if you have the opportunity, the
number one thing you should bring is a camera

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:36.720
to capture the stunning landscapes and distinctive
towns.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:40.480
Before heading off, though, you should brush
up on your photography skills with Skillshare’s

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:41.930
landscape photography class.

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:46.300
Using their iOS or Android apps, you can even
download the classes offline for the flight.

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:50.320
Of course, to go the Arctic you first need
the time so, in order to streamline your work

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:54.500
processes and improve your productivity, you
should also take their “Productivity Masterclass”

00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:58.050
course by fellow YouTuber and all-around nice
guy Thomas Frank.

00:11:58.050 --> 00:12:02.690
In addition to these two, Skillshare has about
22,000 courses so it’s the perfect place

00:12:02.690 --> 00:12:04.910
to go for those with varied interests.

00:12:04.910 --> 00:12:08.720
Best of all, you can take either and all of
